      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Electrical stimulation of medial brainstem sites produces potent analgesia in rats that is either opioid- or non-opioid-mediated depending on the specific brain region stimulated. Footshock stress also causes opioid and non-opioid forms of analgesia in rats depending on the exact parameters of footshock administered. We now report that opioid, but not non-opioid, stress analgesia demonstrates cross-tolerance with opioid, but not non-opioid, stimulation-produced analgesia. This finding suggests that opioid forms of stimulation-produced and stress-induced analgesia share a common substrate."},
